# Source: HoareLogic.pdf, section "Exercises", "A Series of Assignments".
id: series-of-assignments
title: A series of assignments
topic: Sequential composition
level: 1
source: HoareLogic.pdf, Exercises, "A Series of Assignments"
tags: assignment, sequence, wp, intervals

task:
  The program computes a result in the variable $res$ from $x$ and $y$.

  Calculate the weakest precondition of the program for the postcondition
  $Q$, statement by statement from the last one. Then use the consequence
  rule to show that the triple is valid.

pre: -10 <= x <= 0 && 0 <= y <= 5
post: -10 <= res <= 10

program:
  {P}
  {R3}
  y := y + 10;     // i1
  {R2}
  x := x - 5;      // i2
  {R1}
  res := x + y     // i3
  {Q}

verdict: valid

step: R1, the weakest precondition of res := x + y
rule: ASSIGN, WP
focus: i3
text:
  The precondition of an assignment is obtained by substituting the
  assigned variable ($res$) with the assignment expression ($x + y$) in
  the postcondition $Q$.
calc:
  wp(res := x + y, Q)
  = (-10 <= res <= 10)[res <- x + y]
  = -10 <= x + y <= 10         // res is replaced by x + y
set: R1 = -10 <= x + y <= 10

step: R2, the weakest precondition of x := x - 5
rule: ASSIGN, WP
focus: i2
text:
  We substitute the assigned variable ($x$) with the assignment expression
  ($x - 5$) in the postcondition of this statement, which is $R1$.
calc:
  wp(x := x - 5, R1)
  = (-10 <= x + y <= 10)[x <- x - 5]
  = -10 <= (x - 5) + y <= 10   // x is replaced by x - 5
  = -10 <= x + y - 5 <= 10     // remove the parentheses and reorder the sum
  = -5 <= x + y <= 15          // add 5 to the three parts
set: R2 = -5 <= x + y <= 15

step: R3, the weakest precondition of y := y + 10
rule: ASSIGN, WP
focus: i1
text:
  We substitute the assigned variable ($y$) with the assignment expression
  ($y + 10$) in the postcondition of this statement, which is $R2$.
calc:
  wp(y := y + 10, R2)
  = (-5 <= x + y <= 15)[y <- y + 10]
  = -5 <= x + (y + 10) <= 15   // y is replaced by y + 10
  = -5 <= x + y + 10 <= 15     // remove the parentheses
  = -15 <= x + y <= 5          // subtract 10 from the three parts
set: R3 = -15 <= x + y <= 5

step: P implies R3
rule: CONSEQUENCE
focus: P, R3
text:
  $R3$ is the weakest precondition of the whole program for $Q$. By the
  consequence rule, the triple is valid when the precondition $P$ implies
  $R3$.
  >>> «P» ==> «R3»
vc: -10 <= x <= 0 && 0 <= y <= 5 ==> -15 <= x + y <= 5
why:
  We add the bounds that $P$ gives for $x$ and $y$. The smallest value of
  $x + y$ is $-10 + 0 = -10$ and the largest is $0 + 5 = 5$. So
  $-10 <= x + y <= 5$, and $[-10, 5]$ is included in $[-15, 5]$.
result: The triple is valid.

summary:
  Going backwards, each assignment shifts the interval of $x + y$:
  $[-10, 10]$, then $[-5, 15]$, then $[-15, 5]$. The precondition keeps
  $x + y$ in $[-10, 5]$, which is inside $[-15, 5]$.
